backups.append(dict(title='Could shorter answers explain output-control transfer?',kicker='TRAINING COT CONTROL TEACHES OUTPUT CONTROL',headers=['GPT-OSS training','Strict output\ncompliance','Strict + >5 words\n(same denominator)','Median output\nwords'],rows=[[label,f'{T[r]["rate"]*100:.1f}%',f'{T[r]["strict_over5words"]*100:.1f}%',f'{T[r]["median_words"]:g}'] for r,label in [('base','Base'),('grpo3','Six CoT rules'),('lang1-s40','Language, s40'),('len2','Token budget')]],bullets=['Six-rule and language models have similar median output lengths to base; the token-budget model is shorter.', 'Requiring more than five words leaves most of the gain intact. This does not control for the full length distribution.'],notes='The output-control evaluation forces the analysis channel empty, asks for an answer and explanation, and applies the rule to the entire visible response. All four arms use the same 480 items, with repeated samples averaged within each item. The >5-word check keeps every item in the denominator and counts short responses as failures; it therefore tests whether very short outputs account for the observed gains. The six-rule improvement is 36.3 percentage points, with a paired 95% question-cluster bootstrap interval of 32.8 to 39.8 points. This does not isolate channel-specific learning because training updated the full generated sequence and included final-answer feedback.',caption='480 matched items; 413 question clusters; meta discussion allowed; answer correctness is not required. Checkpoints and source data match the main output-control draft.'))
qualityruns=['qwen38-run2','opsd-grpo1-step30-t1','inksmtk-up-e06','inkrl1-s80-req-e06','inksmtk-up','inkrl1-s80-req-e09']
qualitylabels=['Qwen · base','Qwen · OPSD + RL','Inkling 0.6 · base','Inkling 0.6 · SFT + RL','Inkling 0.9 · base','Inkling 0.9 · SFT + RL']
backups.append(dict(title='Does compliance preserve correct answers?',kicker='QWEN AND INKLING CONTROLLERS',headers=['Model / effort','Strict','Correct + valid\n+ compliant','Correct answers'],rows=[[label]+[f'{A[r][k]}/96' for k in ['strict','joint','correct']] for r,label in zip(qualityruns,qualitylabels)],bullets=['Joint success rises, while answer accuracy under a constraint falls.', 'Inkling’s higher thinking effort trades some compliance for higher answer accuracy.'],notes='These are the same 96 deterministic Astra items, with one generation per item. Joint success requires strict compliance, a nonempty closed thinking block, no length truncation, a parseable final answer, and the saved correct answer. Syntactic validity and a correct answer do not establish that the text contains substantive reasoning: sampled OPSD passes include “solve.” and repeated meows. Inkling s80 uses effort 0.6 on the main slide; the matched 0.9 comparison is included here to expose the effort dependence. Checkpoints were evaluated during recipe development, and these results do not measure variation across training seeds.',caption='Raw deterministic regrade and independent final-answer extraction. All cells retain invalid or failed generations in the denominator.'))
backups.append(dict(title='How much of DeepSeek’s evaluation was held out?',kicker='DEEPSEEK REWRITE SFT',headers=['Check','What the audit establishes'],rows=[['Instruction families','Happy and French absent from both RL and SFT'],['SFT question exposure','0 of 60 eval questions in actual SFT examples'],['Earlier RL pool','1 of 60 eval questions in the eligible RL pool'],['Exclude that question','Sad 47/59; story 27/59; happy 46/59; French 16/59']],bullets=['The style transfer survives excluding the known overlapping question.', 'This is an exact-text overlap audit; near-duplicates and actual earlier RL sampling remain unchecked.'],notes='The comparison starts from an already RL-trained DeepSeek controller, so a style must be absent from both stages to count as untrained. Happy and French satisfy that check; pirate, misdirect, answer-hiding, and ignore-question already appeared in preceding RL prompts. The dedicated 60-problem evaluation shares no question with the actual rewrite-SFT examples, but one Philena/Nathan problem is a punctuation-normalized duplicate in the eligible RL pool. All four highlighted before-SFT pass counts remain zero after excluding it, and all four after-SFT numerators are unchanged. The broader Astra and original transfer sets have more overlap for this DeepSeek lineage, so the main slide uses the dedicated evaluation.',caption='Question overlap: normalized-text audit and actual SFT exposure check. RL pool membership does not prove that the question was sampled into an update.'))
backups.append(dict(title='Where does DeepSeek succeed, and what does it cost?',kicker='DEEPSEEK REWRITE SFT',headers=['Request','Passes\nbefore → after','Correct + passing\nafter','Correct answers\nbefore → after'],rows=[[label,f'{S[t]["before"]["pass"]} → {S[t]["after"]["pass"]}',str(S[t]['after']['joint']),f'{S[t]["before"]["correct"]} → {S[t]["after"]["correct"]}'] for t,label in [('sad','Sad'),('cover_story','Cover story'),('happy','Happy'),('french','French'),('misdirect','Misdirect'),('answer_hiding','Answer hiding')]],bullets=['Misdirect and answer hiding still have zero passes.', 'No-request accuracy: 50/60 → 44/60. Semantic judge calibration remains incomplete.'],notes='Every displayed row uses 60 matched problems; pass requires format validity, no truncation, strict compliance, attempted solving, and no meta discussion. Accuracy and the joint correct-and-passing metric are shown separately. The semantic strict-compliance threshold is 10/10, and the labels come from the stored DeepSeek judge; recounting them validates arithmetic rather than judge correctness. Thirty random before/after records were sampled for excerpt review, including positive and negative pass labels, but this is not full-trace independent semantic calibration. The complete recorded control table follows.\n\n'+'\n'.join(f'{t}: N={x["n"]}, passes {x["before"]["pass"]}→{x["after"]["pass"]}, correct {x["before"]["correct"]}→{x["after"]["correct"]}, joint after={x["after"]["joint"]}' for t,x in S.items() if t!='baseline'),caption='All displayed cells are counts out of 60. A pass does not require a correct final answer. Full style table and sources are in speaker notes and the audit files.'))
